column reads back as strings, with the missing value as \code{NA}.
\strong{A factor written by \code{arrow::write_parquet()} reads as a string
column}: arrow keeps the levels in the parquet dictionary pages, which
libcudf does not expose. If you need the factor, convert it afterwards, e.g.
\code{mutate(f = factor(f, levels = ...))} on the collected data.
