are not pushed: libcudf 25.12 can skip a row group wrongly from a double
column's \code{NaN} statistics or a string column's deprecated min/max statistics
(signed byte order), which would lose rows. They are applied after the read, so a filter on doubles or
strings gives the same rows but skips no row groups. A factor written by
\code{arrow::write_parquet()} reads as a string (see above), so it is not pushed
either.
Everything else (\code{|}, \code{!}, \code{is.na()}, \code{\%in\%}, arithmetic, functions, a double
literal against an \code{integer64} column, a column that was converted on
reading such as a factor, a filter on a grouped table, with \code{.by}, or on a
rowwise table, a filter that comes after a \code{mutate()} that changes its column,
or after a \code{slice()}, \code{distinct()} or \code{summarise()}) is applied after the read,
as without pushdown, and a filter with anything left to apply is applied whole.
The answer is the same either way.
\code{options(cuplyr.parquet_pushdown = FALSE)} turns both pushdowns off.

A file whose footer statistics are malformed (for example a fixed-width
statistic of the wrong length; an invalid file that no common writer
produces) can crash the R session when a filter is pushed into the scan. For
files you don't trust, \code{options(cuplyr.parquet_pushdown = FALSE)} avoids it.
